<section class="project-story"><p class="eyebrow">THE IDEA</p><h2>What if the bear<br> could talk back?</h2><div class="prose"><p>Bear 5 is my Costco plush bear robotics project. The first goal is simple to describe: give the bear a voice and a way to have a conversation.</p><p>The starting idea is a Raspberry Pi 5 paired with an edge model. Gemma 4 is one candidate to evaluate; the model choice and what runs well on the hardware still need exploring.</p><p>Once conversation is working, the next chapter is sensing: helping the bear notice a little more of the world around it.</p></div></section>
<section class="roadmap" aria-labelledby="roadmap-title"><div class="section-heading"><h2 id="roadmap-title">One small experiment at a time.</h2><span class="hand-note">a rough trail map</span></div><ol><li><span class="step-number">01</span><div><span class="eyebrow">FIRST CHAPTER / PLANNED</span><h3>Give the bear a voice.</h3><p>Explore Raspberry Pi 5, a suitable edge model, and the pieces needed for a conversation.</p></div></li><li><span class="step-number">02</span><div><span class="eyebrow">NEXT CHAPTER / LATER</span><h3>Let the bear sense its world.</h3><p>Build on the conversation experiments with sensing, guided by what the first chapter teaches us.</p></div></li></ol></section>
<aside class="margin-note"><span class="hand-note">The experiment is the interesting part.</span><p>Build notes, discoveries, and the inevitable detours will be collected here as the project develops.</p></aside>
